Setting (continued from the previous page). We have morphisms X --f--> S --> T and the relative de Rham Leray spectral sequence H^*_DR(X/T) <= E_2^{pq} = H^p(S, alpha -> H^q(Omega^alpha_{S/T} (x)^L Rf_*Omega^._{X/S})). The differential comes from the Gauss-Manin connection. When Omega^1_{S/T} is flat, E_2^{pq} = H^p_DR(S/T, R^q f_*Omega^._{X/S}); this is essentially the Katz-Oda spectral sequence.

Class under study. Take xi in H^n_DR(X/T) whose image in H^0(S, H^n_DR(X/S)) (the edge map) vanishes. This image is automatically horizontal. So xi lies in F^1 of the Leray filtration and defines an element of E_inf^{1,n-1}. Nothing comes into E^{1,n-1}, since E^{-1,*} = 0, so E_inf^{1,n-1} is a subobject of E_2^{1,n-1}. Hence xi gives a class
- (15) eta in H^1(S, alpha -> H^{n-1}(Omega^alpha_{S/T} (x)^L Rf_*Omega_{X/S}));
- (16) under flatness of Omega^1_{S/T}, eta in H^1_DR(S/T, R^{n-1}f_*Omega_{X/S}).

Torsor description. Grothendieck then recalls that an H^1 of the de Rham complex of a module with integrable connection (H, nabla) classifies isomorphism classes of pairs (P, d_P):
- P is an H-torsor;
- d_P: P -> Omega^1_{S/T} (x) H is affine over nabla, i.e. d_P(x+s) = d_P(x) + nabla(s);
- d_P takes values in closed 1-forms Z^1(Omega^._{S/T} (x) H), the integrability condition.

This is an 'affine integrable connection on a torsor'. Equivalently it is an extension 0 -> H -> E -> O_S -> 0 of modules with integrable connection. Here H = R^{n-1}f_*, the de Rham cohomology of the fibres, with its Gauss-Manin connection.

Characteristic-zero variant (bracketed remark). Without flatness hypotheses, and in characteristic 0, d_P is equivalent to an affine stratification delta_P^inf: P -> P^inf_{S/T}(H)^+. Here:
- P^inf_{S/T}(H) are the principal parts of infinite order and ^+ is the augmentation-ideal part;
- delta_P^inf(x+s) = delta_P^inf(x) + d^+(s), with d^+ = d^inf - delta^inf;
- d^inf is the canonical Taylor map and delta^inf comes from the stratification of H attached to the Gauss-Manin connection.

This is the torsor (affine) version of the equivalence 'integrable connection <=> stratification' in characteristic 0 from Grothendieck's 'Crystals and the de Rham cohomology of schemes'. Grothendieck marks it with a '?'.

Purpose. A cohomology class that vanishes fibrewise yields an extension of the Gauss-Manin system of the fibres' H^{n-1} by O_S. This is the algebraic de Rham / D-module analogue of Griffiths' normal function, or of the extension of variations of Hodge structure attached to a cycle class homologous to zero on the fibres.
